  <section class="block" id="prompt">
    <div class="kicker">Build yours today</div>
    <h2>Start with a prompt.</h2>
    <p class="sub">The kit you can copy is still in progress. In the meantime, this prompt has an AI assistant interview you first, then build a small monitor that fits your site.</p>
    <div class="prompt-grid">
      <div class="prompt-steps">
        <div class="step-card">
          <div class="step-num">STEP 1</div>
          <h3>Copy the prompt.</h3>
          <p>One click. Nothing to fill in yet.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="step-card">
          <div class="step-num">STEP 2</div>
          <h3>Paste it into a coding assistant.</h3>
          <p>One that can create and run files, like Claude Code, opened in a new empty folder.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="step-card">
          <div class="step-num">STEP 3</div>
          <h3>Answer its questions.</h3>
          <p>It asks about your site first, explains what it will build, and waits for your OK.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="prompt-note"><span class="chip nottested">Not tested yet</span> I wrote this from what my own monitor does. I have not run this exact prompt start to finish on a fresh site yet. If you try it, <a href="/contact">tell me how it went</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="prompt-wrap">
        <button class="btn primary copy-btn" type="button" id="copy-monitor-prompt">Copy prompt</button>
        <pre class="promptbox" id="monitor-prompt">I want you to help me build my own website health monitor: a small tool I own that checks my site the way a visitor sees it and gives me a red, yellow and green report with a plain list of what to fix.

Before you write any code, interview me. Ask these one at a time and wait for each answer. Keep it plain, I am not a developer.
1. What is my website address? Do I have more than one site to watch?
2. What is the site for right now: a business card, a place to book or buy, a blog, or something else? This changes what counts as broken.
3. How often do I expect to update it: weekly, monthly or rarely?
4. Do I want a check I run whenever I like, or a daily check that runs on its own? If daily, do I have a free GitHub account?
5. Do I use visitor stats, like GoatCounter or Google Analytics, and do I want those numbers in the report?
6. What computer do I use, Windows or Mac, and have I ever used a terminal?
7. Has anything broken on my site before that I want watched closely?

Then tell me in plain words what you will build, and wait for my OK.

When you build it, follow these rules:
- Check what a visitor actually receives, not only the status code. A page can answer 200 OK and still be broken. Confirm images and files really load and are the right type, a missing page returns a real 404 instead of the homepage, and the social sharing image (og:image) exists and is an image.
- Also check broken links inside and outside the site, page titles and descriptions, robots.txt, sitemap.xml and a favicon. If I told you how often I update, note when the site's text last changed.
- Sort every finding into red (a visitor would notice), yellow (fix soon) or info (nothing to do). Explain each one in a plain sentence and list the pages it affects.
- Write the report as one simple HTML page I can open in a browser, with the top three things to fix at the top.
- Be polite to websites: only check my own sites, identify the tool with a plain user agent, keep the page count small and pause between requests.
- Never put passwords, API keys or tokens in files I might share or commit. If my visitor stats need a key, show me how to store it safely.
- Keep the code small and readable, with comments a beginner can follow, and tell me exactly how to run it again.
- Do not change my website. If you find problems, list them and wait. I review every change before anything goes live.</pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

